headings answers a heading selection with every match's file path, heading text, heading identifier, level, and the span of the content beneath it. The selection is one operand written <document>#<heading>, either half of which may be omitted: <document> alone selects every heading in that document, #<heading> selects that heading in every corpus member, and an absent operand selects every heading in the corpus. A document half holding a path separator or ending in .md is read as a repository-relative path, and otherwise as a slug. A heading is matched on its identifier, so letter case and punctuation do not decide a match. The heading half may be a path of headings separated by #, outermost first, each nested within the one before it at any depth, so <document>#Components#References selects the References heading beneath Components; every match carries its anchor and a ref, which pastes back as a selection, naming that heading alone unless the anchor is a single segment another heading shares. A matched heading is returned with every heading nested inside its section. Given --emit, the content the one matching heading stands over is written as itself instead; --emit requires the selection to match exactly one heading, and a selection matching several is refused with each match named: those a selection matches alone as selections to choose from, and the rest as reachable only through the span the query without --emit returns. A selection with no heading half matches every heading of the document, so a caller after a whole document reads the file.

Usage:
  specs headings [<document>#<heading>] [flags]

Flags:
      --emit   write the content the one matching heading stands over, instead of the match
  -h, --help   help for headings
      --json   write the matches as JSON instead of YAML

Global Flags:
      --config string   path to specs.toml (default: the nearest specs.toml in the working directory or above it, up to the repository root)
